# A `T?` is a by-value struct, so it is TRANSPARENT to containment: it propagates
# the edge into `T` rather than breaking it. `next: Node?` has no size.
#
# This row is here because two panel-023 judges found it independently, from
# opposite ends and with different inputs. The llm-ergonomist, reading only the
# spec, named `next: Node?` "the single most attractive wrong answer" for a linked
# list — spec line 53 says "Absence is a different type (`T?`)" while line 50
# defines a `T?` as "a `T`, or an error", so the spec itself licenses `T?` as an
# option type. The compiler-engineer, reading only the tree, found the same shape
# as a representation defect. Then the ffi-pragmatist compiled it: `field has
# incomplete type 'h_m_R'`, exit 1.
#
# The proposal's rule — "a cycle not through `[T]` or `{K: V}`" — would have
# PERMITTED this program, and the error the author would have seen names a struct
# the compiler invented, with no line pointing at this file. Which is why the rule
# is a property and not a list: an edge is a field C lays out by value.
#
# Measured alongside it, and all three compile: `record P { kids: [P?] }` is 8
# bytes, `record Q { kids: [Q]? }` is 40, `record S { m: {i64: S?} }` is 8. The
# `T?` is not the problem — being reached by value is.

record Node
    value: i64
    next: Node?    #~ no_size

function main()
    print(1)
